              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you regarding your patient, a 32-year-old male, admitted on 27th January 1997 with acute right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. Clinical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Laboratory investigations confirmed leukocytosis, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids and commenced on intravenous antibiotics. Following anaesthetic assessment, he underwent an uncomplicated open appendectomy on 28th January 1997. Histopathological examination of the resected specimen confirmed acute appendicitis without perforation. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. The patient was mobilised early, tolerating oral intake well, and was discharged home on 31st January 1997 with oral analgesia and a course of antibiotics. Wound review is recommended in 7–10 days. Please do not hesitate to contact us should any concerns arise.</div>"
            },
